# ============================================================================
# gc-wiki MCP route — insert into ~/.config/caddy-gc/Caddyfile
# IMMEDIATELY ABOVE the final `handle { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 }`
# inside the gcworkflow.clemson.edu:8443 site block.
# ============================================================================
# WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-09-07, raised by the gcdept_agents session):
# /api/mcp is not claimed by any explicit route — verified by reading the
# Caddyfile: it reaches the wiki only through the final catch-all. Migrating
# eight faculty agent groups onto a fallback path is precisely the trap this
# Caddyfile already records, where /cu_catalog fell through to the capture tool
# and returned a look-alike auth error. So the wiki gets its own prefix:
#   https://gcworkflow.clemson.edu:8443/gc_wiki/
#
# WHY `rewrite` AND NOT `handle_path` (the non-obvious part):
# the cu_* MCP servers ignore the request path, so stripping their prefix is
# safe. This backend is a Next app and IS path-sensitive. Measured against
# 127.0.0.1:3000 on 2026-09-07 and independently reconfirmed here:
#     POST /api/mcp   → 401   ← the real MCP endpoint (bearer required)
#     POST /          → 200   ← HTML app shell
#     POST /gc_wiki/  → 308
# So a plain `handle_path /gc_wiki/*` would strip the prefix, land on the app
# root, and return a cheerful 200 that is not MCP at all. Rewriting every
# /gc_wiki* path to /api/mcp is what actually reaches the server.
#
# WHY `flush_interval -1`:
# MCP replies are text/event-stream; a buffering proxy holds streamed
# notifications. Copied from the working /cu_schedule block rather than
# rediscovered.
#
# ORDERING: `handle` blocks are evaluated in source order, so this must sit
# ABOVE the catch-all or the catch-all wins and nothing changes.
# ============================================================================

	handle /gc_wiki* {
		rewrite * /api/mcp
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
